  - create a plan first to fix the issue.
  - then fix it, verify the code. no need linting o phpcs checking , skip them.
  - verify the issue is properly fixed by running the browser test agin.
  - after it's fixed, move it to closed. if not fixed and cant fix yet, move it to blocked.


## agent-browser Usage

Vercel's agent-browser is the single tool for browser automation, QA verification, and any browser navigation. It is a native CLI driving Chrome/Chromium over CDP and manages its own browser, so no manual Chrome launch or sandbox flags are needed.

Setup:

- Install once: `npm i -g agent-browser && agent-browser install`.
- Load the version-matched guide before using it: `agent-browser skills get core` (overview + common patterns) or `agent-browser skills get core --full` (full command reference). For exploratory testing and bug hunts load `agent-browser skills get dogfood`; run `agent-browser skills list` for the rest.

Core loop:

```bash
agent-browser open https://mirror-help.arrayhash.com/wp-admin   # navigate
agent-browser snapshot -i                                       # interactive elements + @eN refs
agent-browser click @e3                                         # act on a ref
agent-browser snapshot -i                                       # re-snapshot after any change
agent-browser screenshot --full /tmp/state.png                 # capture to judge the UI
```

Rules:

- Refs (`@e1`, `@e2`, …) are reassigned on every snapshot and go stale after navigation, form submits, or re-renders — always re-snapshot before the next ref interaction.
- Use isolated sessions for different auth contexts: `agent-browser --session admin …`, `--session customer …`, `--session guest …`. Persist or restore login with `--session-name`, `state save`/`state load`, or the auth vault as needed.
- Prefer precise waits over fixed sleeps: `wait @ref`, `wait --text "…"`, `wait --url "**/wp-admin"`, `wait --load networkidle`.
- Capture before/after/error screenshots and base UI judgments on those plus snapshot, DOM, and network observations (`network requests`, `console`, `errors`).
- Close sessions when done: `agent-browser close --all`.

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### Plugin Ownership

- Put shared subscription engine behavior, free modules, shared lifecycle rules, shared support classes, and shared contracts in `arraysubs`.
- Put premium-only modules, premium UI, premium automations, premium integrations, and premium workflows in `arraysubspro`.
- If a feature spans both plugins:
  1. Add the reusable contract or extension point in `arraysubs`.
  2. Add the premium implementation in `arraysubspro`.
  3. Verify core still works correctly without pro active.

### Cross-Plugin Alignment

When changing shared behavior, review both plugins for:

- hooks and filters
- helper functions and service responsibilities
- REST routes, schemas, and payload shapes
- post meta, options, transients, status names, and table names
- Action Scheduler hooks, groups, and arguments
- asset handles, admin routes, and runtime data objects
- subscription lifecycle, billing, renewal, grace-period, and cancellation flows

Prefer backward-compatible changes. Do not silently break `arraysubspro` through core refactors.

### Shared Support And Config

- Reuse shared support classes from `ArraySubs\Supports\...`.
- Use the core `ArraySubs\Supports\Config` store.
- Shared runtime values stay under existing core scopes such as `plugin.*`, `wp.*`, and `settings.*`.
- Add only pro-specific runtime values under `proplugin.*`.
- Do not create a second shared support layer in `arraysubspro`.

### Subscription Data Standard

- Treat subscriptions as `WP_Post` objects, not arrays.
- Use `arraysubs_get_subscription()` to retrieve subscriptions.
- Read subscription meta with `get_post_meta($subscription->ID, ...)`.
- When firing subscription-related hooks, pass a `WP_Post` object as the subscription payload.
- Handlers should stay defensive for backward compatibility, but new code must use `WP_Post`.

### Action Scheduler Standard

- Use the centralized `ArraySubs\Supports\ActionScheduler` class.
- Use defined `HOOK_*` and `GROUP_*` constants, never raw strings.
- Pass indexed arrays for scheduler args, not associative arrays.
- Use scheduler locks in handlers to prevent duplicate concurrent work.
- Keep new scheduled actions aligned with the existing registration and unscheduling patterns.
- Renewal scheduling uses both `HOOK_GENERATE_RENEWAL_INVOICE` for precise per-subscription invoice generation and `HOOK_PROCESS_RENEWAL` for due-time payment processing; keep both actions aligned when changing renewal dates.

### Date And Time Formatting

Display-only dates must use the shared helpers from `arraysubs/src/functions/date-calculations.php`:

- `arraysubs_format_date_local()` for UTC-stored datetimes
- `arraysubs_format_site_local_date()` for site-local datetimes
- `arraysubs_format_timestamp_local()` for Unix timestamps

Do not use those helpers for scheduling logic. Scheduling should continue to use timestamps, `DateTime`, and raw stored values as appropriate.

### UI Confirmation, Prompt, And Loading Standard (CRITICAL)

Native browser dialogs (`window.alert`, `window.confirm`, `window.prompt`) are **forbidden** anywhere in this codebase. Every action button that triggers an AJAX/REST call MUST also surface a visible loading state. Use the project's shared helpers — never roll your own.

#### Admin React (`arraysubs/src/resources/`, `arraysubspro/src/resources/`)

Confirmation modals — use `useConfirm()` from `@libs/confirm-dialog`:

```jsx
import { useConfirm } from "@libs/confirm-dialog";
import SpinnerButton from "@libs/confirm-dialog/SpinnerButton";

const MyComponent = () => {
  const { confirm, confirmDialog } = useConfirm();
  const [processing, setProcessing] = useState(false);

  const handleDangerousAction = async () => {
    try {
      await confirm({
        title: __("Cancel Subscription", "arraysubs"),
        message: __("This cannot be undone. Continue?", "arraysubs"),
        confirmText: __("Cancel Subscription", "arraysubs"),
        cancelText: __("Keep Subscription", "arraysubs"),
        loadingText: __("Cancelling…", "arraysubs"),
        variant: "danger", // "info" | "warning" | "danger"
        onConfirm: async () => {
          setProcessing(true);
          try {
            // do the AJAX work — modal stays open with spinner until this resolves
          } finally {
            setProcessing(false);
          }
        },
      });
    } catch (error) {
      showToast(error.message, "error");
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <SpinnerButton
        className="button arraysubs-btn--danger"
        loading={processing}
        loadingText={__("Cancelling…", "arraysubs")}
        onClick={handleDangerousAction}
      >
        {__("Cancel Subscription", "arraysubs")}
      </SpinnerButton>

      {confirmDialog}{/* MUST be mounted in the JSX tree */}
    </>
  );
};
```

Rules:

- Mount `{confirmDialog}` in the component's JSX tree (typically right before `<ToastContainer>`).
- Pick the right `variant`: `danger` for destructive (cancel, delete), `warning` for risky/irreversible state changes (status flip, reset), `info` for benign confirmations (resume, undo).
- When the modal's `onConfirm` runs an async operation, the modal stays open with its spinner until the promise resolves. If it throws, the dialog stays open so the caller can show a toast and the user can retry.
- For success/error feedback after the operation, use `useToast` (`@libs/toast`) — never `alert`.
- Every page that uses `useConfirm()` and calls REST endpoints should wire `useToast` so error toasts appear.

Action button loading state — use `<SpinnerButton>` from `@libs/confirm-dialog/SpinnerButton`:

- Drop-in replacement for `<button>`. Pass `loading={state}` and optional `loadingText` (defaults to `"Wait…"`).
- Renders `<Loader2 className="arraysubs-spin" />` next to the loading text while `loading={true}`. Disabled is implicit.
- Use it everywhere a click triggers an AJAX call — both inside modals and on standalone page buttons.

DataList errors — pass `showToast` to `<DataList>`:

- `<DataList showToast={showToast} ... />` — without it the lib falls back to `console.error` (no user-visible feedback). Always pass it.

#### Customer Portal jQuery (`arraysubs/src/resources/customerPortal.js`, `arraysubspro/src/resources/customerPortalPro.js`)

Use the helpers from `./customer-portal/uiHelpers` (free) or `./customer-portal-pro/uiHelpers` (pro):

```js
import {
  confirmDialog,
  promptDialog,
  portalToast,
  setButtonLoading,
} from "./customer-portal/uiHelpers";

// Confirmation
const ok = await confirmDialog({
  title: "Cancel Subscription",
  message: "Are you sure?",
  confirmText: "Cancel Subscription",
  cancelText: "Keep",
  variant: "danger",
  // Optional: if onConfirm is provided, the dialog's confirm button shows a
  // spinner while it runs and stays open if it throws.
  onConfirm: async () => { /* ajax work */ },
});
if (!ok) return;

// Input prompt (replaces native window.prompt)
const value = await promptDialog({
  title: "Skip Renewal",
  label: "Cycles to skip",
  inputType: "number",
  min: 1,
  max: 12,
  defaultValue: "1",
  validate: (v) => (v ? null : "Required"),
});
if (value === null) return; // cancelled

// Floating notification (replaces alert for success/error)
portalToast("Subscription cancelled.", "success");
portalToast(errorMsg, "error");

// Action button loading state
setButtonLoading($btn, true, "Cancelling…"); // adds spinner SVG + text, disables button
// ... ajax ...
setButtonLoading($btn, false); // restores original markup
```

Rules:

- Replace every `alert()` with `portalToast()` (success / error / info).
- Replace every `confirm()` with `await confirmDialog({...})`.
- Replace every `prompt()` with `await promptDialog({...})`.
- Wrap AJAX-triggered button clicks with `setButtonLoading($btn, true)` before the call and `setButtonLoading($btn, false)` in the error handler. (Success paths usually reload, so the restore is unnecessary there.)
- After a successful action that reloads the page, use `setTimeout(() => window.location.reload(), 600)` so the toast is visible briefly first.
- Don't drop redundant in-flow native confirms when a UI modal already serves as the confirmation step (e.g. the Cancel Modal IS the confirmation — don't add a `confirmDialog` on top of it).

#### Forbidden everywhere

- ❌ `window.alert(...)`, `alert(...)`
- ❌ `window.confirm(...)`, `confirm(...)`
- ❌ `window.prompt(...)`, `prompt(...)`
- ❌ Action buttons that fire AJAX without a visible loading state (no spinner / no "Wait…" text / no `disabled` while pending)
- ❌ Custom one-off confirmation modals when the shared helpers fit
- ❌ Inline `<style>` blocks for spinner styles — the SCSS already defines `.arraysubs-spin` (admin) and `.arraysubs-portal-spinner` / `.arraysubs-btn--loading` (customer portal)

If the shared helpers genuinely don't fit a use case, escalate before inventing a parallel system.

## Development Standards

### Stack And Patterns

- PHP 8.0+, PSR-4 autoloading, WordPress coding standards
- React 18 with functional components and hooks
- SCSS for styling
- `@wordpress/scripts` for builds
- Lucide React icons only
- Existing libraries and systems such as DataList, FormBuilder, Modal, Toast, ConfirmDialog (`useConfirm` + `SpinnerButton`), MenuConfig, BaseRestController, Config, and ActionScheduler
- Customer portal helpers: `confirmDialog`, `promptDialog`, `portalToast`, `setButtonLoading` from `customer-portal/uiHelpers` (free) and `customer-portal-pro/uiHelpers` (pro)

### Do Not Introduce

- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- jQuery-based UI work when React or vanilla JS fits
- custom routing, settings, form, or scheduler systems
- duplicate core logic in pro
- custom enqueue wrapper abstractions
- native browser dialogs (`alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`) — use the shared `useConfirm` / `confirmDialog` / `promptDialog` / `portalToast` helpers instead
- one-off button loading patterns — use `<SpinnerButton>` (React) or `setButtonLoading()` (jQuery)

### Security

- Add `defined('ABSPATH') || exit;` to PHP entry files.
- Sanitize all inputs.
- Escape all outputs.
- Verify nonces.
- Enforce capability checks.
- Use `$wpdb->prepare()` for database queries.
- Keep all user-facing strings translatable with the correct text domain.

### REST And Frontend Rules

- Settings endpoints remain admin-only.
- Other admin REST endpoints must check `manage_woocommerce` or `manage_options`.
- Customer endpoints must require login and verify ownership via `_customer_id`.
- Use `@libs/url` for frontend URL construction with query parameters.
- Use `wp_add_inline_script()` for runtime data objects when needed.
- Do not use `wp_localize_script()` for arbitrary runtime app state.
- Do not output regular inline `<script>` or `<style>` blocks in PHP templates.

### Large Dataset Selects

For users, products, orders, roles, terms, CPTs, or any other dynamic site data:

- use searchable AJAX-based selects
- load results on demand
- cap the result window to the first 5 pages
- do not preload full datasets

